.TH "UBERTOOTH\-SCAN" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-scan\fR \- active (BlueZ) device scan and inquiry supported by Ubertooth
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
ubertooth\-scan [\-s] [\-x]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
This tool uses a normal Bluetooth dongle to perform Inquiry Scans and Extended Inquiry scans of Bluetooth devices\. It uses Ubertooth to discover undiscoverable devices and can use BlueZ to scan for discoverable devices\.
.P
The Ubertooth capture is gated by the board's squelch, which this tool does not set: it is the default \-120 dBm unless \fBubertooth\-util \-z\fR set another value while the board was idle, and the firmware resets it on every mode change, so it never carries over from an earlier capture\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SQUELCH\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Use Ubertooth to discover devices and perform Inquiry Scan with BlueZ:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-scan
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Use BlueZ and Ubertooth to discover devices and perform Inquiry Scan and Extended Inquiry Scan:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-scan \-s \-x
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-s\fR
hci Scan \- use BlueZ to scan for discoverable devices
.TP
\fB\-x\fR
eXtended scan \- retrieve additional information about target devices
.TP
\fB\-t <seconds>\fR
scan Time \- length of time to sniff packets\. [Default: 20s]
.TP
\fB\-e <0\-5>\fR
Maximum Access Code Errors (default: 2)
.TP
\fB\-b hciN\fR
Bluetooth device (default: hci0)
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
print the usage text on standard output and exit 0\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a bad option value, a stray word or an option given twice is still reported\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-scan\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\.
.P
An option the tool does not know is refused the same way, as one line on standard error with no usage text after it: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR\. An option whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-t' requires a value\fR\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: maximum access code errors must be 0\-5 (got 6)\fR\.
.SH "OUTPUT"
Standard output carries what the scan found\. Nothing the tool itself prints there is anything else, so a run collected in a file or read by a script no longer picks up its headings, its progress or its failures \(em see the end of this section for the two lines libbtbb still adds\.
.P
Each device the BlueZ inquiry of \fB\-s\fR found is its address and its name, one to a line\. While the Ubertooth half runs, each packet it decodes is a line of its own, as \fBubertooth\-rx\fR reports one\. Then each piconet that half found is its address, with the NAP unknown, and the channel map the scan built for it:
.IP "" 4
.nf
systime=1762000000 ch=39 LAP=9e8b33 err=0 clk100ns=1234567 clk1=12 s=\-55 n=\-90 snr=35
??:??:??:9E:8B:33
AFH map: 0x0123456789abcdef1234
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fB\-x\fR adds the extended report of a device under its line, indented, as \fBhcitool info\fR shows one: its address and name, the LMP version and manufacturer, its features, its clock offset, and its channel map as an \fBAFH Map:\fR line, the field name \fBhcitool info\fR gives it\. A device that reports AFH off has no map to report, and standard error carries a \fBnote:\fR line saying so\. A survey result's map is the \fBAFH map:\fR line above, the spelling it has always had\.
.P
A device that does not answer one of those reads has no line for it in the report, and the read that failed is named on standard error: the name, the LMP version, the features and each feature page are left out rather than reported as unknown or, in the features' case, as zero\. Earlier releases left the line out in silence, and printed zeroed features as the device's own\. The device's own line above the report still reads \fB[unknown]\fR when no name comes back, since that line is a result either way\.
.P
Everything else this tool prints goes to standard error: the three headings that say which half of the scan is running (\fBHCI scan\fR, \fBUbertooth scan\fR and \fBScan results:\fR), the \fBRequesting information \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR line of \fB\-x\fR, and every warning and failure (see EXIT STATUS)\. Earlier releases mixed those in with the results, so a scan piped into another program carried them along\.
.P
Some lines on standard output come from libbtbb rather than from this tool, and are still mixed in with the results: what it says while it recovers a piconet's clock, such as \fB12 initial CLK1\-27 candidates\fR, \fBgot CLK1\-27\fR and \fBclock offset = 3\.\fR\. Moving those is #323 \fIhttps://github\.com/SqueakyFloors/ubertooth/issues/323\fR's remaining work, since a library must not write to standard output at all\.
.SH "GAPS IN A CAPTURE"
A capture that is losing packets says so\. The board holds captured packets in a queue until this tool reads them, and a queue with no room left loses the next packet\. The first time it happens the capture warns \fBwarning: the board's packet queue filled up and it dropped at least one packet; this capture has a gap\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many such gaps there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 gaps where the board's queue filled up and it dropped packets\fR\. The count is of gaps, not of lost packets: one full queue can swallow several packets, and the board sends no number\. Nothing says which packets are missing, so a capture with gaps is complete only between them\. The board reports a gap on the first packet that gets through after it, so a capture that ends in the moment after a gap does not report that last one\.
.P
A capture transfer error is reported the same way\. The chip's transfer controller can report an error on the channel a capture receives into: what the board was receiving at the time had arrived, so it is kept and marked, while some of its bytes may be wrong and whatever the fault cut short before it is lost\. The first mark warns \fBwarning: the board had a transfer error while capturing; what it was receiving was kept, but some of those bytes may be wrong and the capture may be missing packets around it\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 transfer errors where a packet may be wrong or missing\fR\. ubertooth\-util(1) \fB\-w\fR reports the board's own count of them since power\-on, over every capture the board has run\.
.P
Symbols the board wrote over are reported the same way\. The board receives into one block of symbols while its capture loop reads the one before it, and when a block finishes arriving before the loop has read that one, the new symbols go over the old ones\. The block is gone before anything looked in it for a packet, so this loss has no packet to point at and nothing in the output marks where it happened\. The first one warns \fBwarning: the board fell behind while capturing and wrote new symbols over a block it had not read yet; whatever was in the air then is missing from this capture\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many such places there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 places where the board wrote new symbols over a block it had not read\fR\. The count is of those places, not of the blocks lost at them: the board has one flag for this and no field to send a number in, so one place can stand for several blocks\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
A scan that ran to its \fB\-t\fR timeout, or that was interrupted (Ctrl\-C), exits 0 with whatever it found, and so does \fB\-h\fR\. A refused command line exits 1 (see ARGUMENTS)\.
.P
A run stopped by Ctrl\-C before it had opened a board exits 0 as well: see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE\.
.P
A scan whose USB stream fails part\-way also exits 1: the board stopped answering, or dropped off the bus, so the Ubertooth half of the scan stops there\. The results it did get are still printed, and the failure is reported on standard error as it happens, as \fBerror: receive transfer failed: <reason>\fR\. A board that never hands the transfer back at all exits 1 too, reported by the \fBwarning:\fR line ubertooth(7) describes rather than that error\.
.P
A BlueZ adapter that is not there, or that is there but will not open, exits 1 with one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error naming the adapter and why, for example \fBerror: Bluetooth device hci7 not found: No such device\fR\. Nothing is printed on standard output, so a caller collecting the scan results in a file does not find the failure among them\.
.P
An inquiry asked for with \fB\-s\fR that the adapter refused exits 1 as well, with \fBerror: the BlueZ inquiry failed: <reason>\fR on standard error: half of what \fB\-s\fR asked for did not happen\. The Ubertooth half still runs \(em it needs no adapter \(em and its results are printed\.
.P
A device the inquiry found that will not take the connection \fB\-x\fR needs, or will not answer one of the reads that fill the report in \(em its name, its LMP version, its features, a features page, its clock offset or its channel map \(em is named the same way and that much of its report is missing\. The scan carries on and prints the rest, and exits 1 because part of what \fB\-x\fR asked for did not happen\. Three failures of that report \(em reading the local adapter's settings, finding memory for the request it makes, and reading the clock offset \(em used to end the whole run instead, with the board still open in whatever mode it was in\.
.P
A piconet the Ubertooth half found is different: a sniffed piconet gives no NAP, so the address \fB\-x\fR connects to has its two most significant bytes guessed as \fB00:00\fR\. Whether that guess stops the connection to a device whose address begins otherwise is unverified: it has not been measured on a bench, and these documents have claimed it both ways (issue #699 \fIhttps://github\.com/SqueakyFloors/ubertooth/issues/699\fR)\. Either way, a connection that cannot be made to such an address is a \fBnote:\fR and the run still exits 0\. Give such a device to \fBubertooth\-scan \-s \-x\fR, or to \fBubertooth\-follow \-t\fR, by its whole address\.
.P
A device that reports AFH off is not such a failure: it answered, and \fBnote: AFH disabled\fR is the answer\. A device that reports an AFH mode byte which is neither off nor on is one, and says what the byte was; it used to be taken for AFH off\.
.P
A capture that could not be started at all exits 1 as well, whichever step failed: the receive transfer libusb would not take, the host thread that collects the capture, or the request that puts the board in its capture mode\. Standard output holds whatever a \fB\-s\fR inquiry printed before that and nothing else; standard error carries the one \fBerror:\fR line for the step that failed\. The capture request is named the way the sweep request below is, with the reason the board gave and the libusb code\. The status is 1 in every case, never the failing call's own return value: a negative libusb code handed to the exit status is wrapped into a byte, so \fBlibusb \-9\fR reached a script as 247\.
.P
A board that refuses the request that puts it in sweep mode exits 1 as well\. Standard output holds whatever a \fB\-s\fR inquiry printed before that and nothing else, not even the heading the Ubertooth results would have gone under\. Standard error names the request, why the board refused it and the libusb code, and adds a note where there is something to try:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: could not set sweep mode: the board did not answer in time (libusb \-7)
note: if a mode is running, stop it with ubertooth\-util \-S; a board that does not answer at all usually resets itself within a few seconds, so run the command again, and unplug it or power\-cycle its hub port if it stays silent
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
That pair is the report\. A sweep the board's firmware has not got is stalled, which reads \fBerror: could not set sweep mode: the board's firmware does not support it (stalled, libusb \-9)\fR, with a note naming the board's firmware API version and the one these tools need (see ubertooth(7), FIRMWARE OF ANOTHER API VERSION)\. The AFH map this tool prints is only meaningful while the board sweeps the whole band, so no scan results are printed after that\. The sweep is refused with the capture already running, so the mode is stopped on the way out, and a board that has stopped answering altogether can add a line of its own to the report as that is done\.
.P
The sweep is stated to the board after the capture has been asked for, because a mode start resets the board's hop mode and would throw away a sweep stated before it: without that, a scan listened to one channel and printed a map that meant nothing\. See ubertooth\-util(1), CARRY\-OVER BETWEEN RUNS\. The board keeps a sweep stated after the capture request whatever it was doing before, so nothing has to be stopped first; a board left in a mode that captures nothing, a killed spectrum scan say, used to swallow it and the map was built from one channel\.
.P
A BlueZ inquiry asked for with \fB\-s\fR runs before the sweep is stated, so its results are printed even when the sweep is then refused: that half of the scan needs no Ubertooth, and what it reported is what it saw\.
.P
A scan the board would not stop exits 1 as well: the request that takes the board out of its capture mode was stalled, so the board is left in that mode and no other tool can open it until it is reset or re\-plugged\. The results the scan found are still printed, and libubertooth names the refused request on standard error\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR reads the attached board, so it exits 1 when a board is attached and cannot be read, or when the USB bus cannot be listed at all; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\.
